00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
experientially-

276
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
That our best scout youth leader training

277
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
is an active process of discovery.

278
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:32,000
In the second part we talked about

279
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,000
the relationships and environment

280
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
most conductive to development,

281
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
and this time around, in this last

282
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
installment, we're going to talk about

283
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,000
specific practices

284
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:44,000
that aid in developing leadership.

285
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:46,000
How do you actually make this happen?

286
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
So I've got a few pieces of advice for you.

287
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:50,000
First, don't wait.

288
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:52,000
If you're going to wait for your scouts to actually

289
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
be leaders before they're trusted

290
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:56,000
with real responsibility,

291
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,000
you're going to be waiting a long time,

292
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:00,000
and so will they.

293
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:02,000
We are developing youth leaders,

294
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:04,000
and youth leaders develop

295
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:06,000
by what-

296
00:11:06,000 --> 00:11:08,000
Experiencing and doing.

297
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:10,000
So we really can't wait until we think they're ready.

298
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:12,000
We'll be developing with them,

299
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,000
we'll be working with them.

300
00:11:14,000 --> 00:11:16,000
things will grow incrementally over time,

301
00:11:16,000 --> 00:11:18,000
but we have to start,

302
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:20,000
And once we do start,

303
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:22,000
we've got to back off and let them lead.

304
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
We don't want to shout at them

305
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,000
or do a whole lot of hand holding.

306
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
we don't want to dog their tracks.

307
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,000
we want to make our advice and mentoring brief,

308
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:32,000
and it's important to realize it'll be more effective

309
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:34,000
if we make fewer statements.

310
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:36,000
this is how you should do this

311
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
and ask a lot more questions:

312
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Well, why did you do it that way?

313
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:42,000
That's the kind of thing that we're looking for.

314
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:44,000
We're looking for an approach to this

315
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,000
with other adults in advisory roles.

316
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,000
it's a good idea, for instance,

317
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
to agree that only one

318
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:52,000
of the adult leaders

319
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
is going to direct the senior patrol leader

320
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
and that should be the scout master.

321
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:58,000
so if anybody else,

322
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,000
if any other adults, have concerns about what's going on,

323
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,000
they need to go to the

324
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,000
scout master or the other designated adult

325
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
and decide how it will be addressed.

326
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:08,000
I've seen

327
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,000
a group of adults,

328
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:12,000
a poor senior patrol leader,

329
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,000
ragged on a weekend

330
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
camping by being helpful,

331
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
by getting lots of directions and advice

332
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
and everything like that, naturally,

333
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,000
and then he gets all this conflicting advice

334
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:24,000
and everything. so we have to coordinate

335
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,000
with other adult leaders and say:

336
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:28,000
look, I'll be talking to the senior

337
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:30,000
patrol leader,

338
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,000
I'll be talking to the patrol leaders,

339
00:12:32,000 --> 00:12:34,000
I'll be doing what's needed there.

340
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:36,000
if you have concerns, bring them to me first.

341
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:38,000
don't give the other youth leaders instructions.

342
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:40,000
we've discussed it so that

343
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:42,000
we're coordinated.

344
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:44,000
one of the other really practical things to do

345
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:46,000
is to respond to initiative.

346
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
respond to initiative from

347
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,000
youth leaders rather than

348
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:52,000
having youth leaders respond

349
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:54,000
to your initiative.

350
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
let them have their own ideas,

351
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:58,000
let them instigate

352
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
their own actions and then

353
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
respond to those, rather than

354
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:04,000
you having all the ideas.

355
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:06,000
if you ask the right questions,

356
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:08,000
you can give them ideas.

357
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:10,000
what are you doing now?

358
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:12,000
what's your job?

359
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:14,000
what's your goal here?

360
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:16,000
what's happening next?

361
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:18,000
they'll put the pieces together

362
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,000
slowly at first, but then they'll begin doing it on their own,

363
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
even newly minted youth leaders.

364
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
they will show initiative

365
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
if we learn to look for it,

366
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
look for the least little signs of it-

367
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,000
an idea, an action that shows promise-

368
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:32,000
and support it.

369
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:34,000
here's some typical expressions of initiative.

370
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:36,000
so if a patrol leader calls all his scouts

371
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:40,000
and they show up at that week's meeting,

372
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:42,000
that's a very big deal

373
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
and you need to fall

374
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,000
over yourself complimenting them about that,

375
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
because they know that they took an action,

376
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,000
they took an initiative

377
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:52,000
and it bore results

378
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:54,000
and that that was a good thing.

379
00:13:54,000 --> 00:13:56,000
so you need to connect those things together for them.

380
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:58,000
let's say, the senior patrol leader

381
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,000
leads a patrol leader's council session

382
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
on their own for the first time.

383
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:04,000
very big deal,

384
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:06,000
very big deal.

385
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:08,000
you need to congratulate your senior patrol leader about that.

386
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
even if it was a giant mess, he did it.

387
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
it was his job and he did it.

388
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
he showed some initiative.

389
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:16,000
when a patrol shops for food

390
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:18,000
and packs it along and prepares it to go on a camping trip,

391
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:20,000
you need to make sure that you're complimentary

392
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:22,000
about it, that you pointed out,

393
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:24,000
and that you celebrate the fact that they actually made it happen.

394
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
now, that is something

395
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,000
that is a fairly common occurrence.

396
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,000
patrols make their own menu,

397
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:32,000
they shop, they get the food, they take it on the trip,

398
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
and it's so common I think

399
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:36,000
that sometimes we forget

400
00:14:36,000 --> 00:14:38,000
what an important developmental step it is.

401
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
we need to recognize that,

402
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
we need to be on top of it,

403
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
we need to respond to that initiative.

404
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:46,000
a meeting or a camp out

405
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,000
or some kind of activity goes by without any adult involvement

406
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:50,000
in the program. we need to respond to that

407
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:52,000
and we need to make sure that they know

408
00:14:52,000 --> 00:14:54,000
how well they did

409
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
so. the things that seem routine to us

410
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:58,000
but are new to youth leaders,

411
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:00,000
these are things that we can look at

412
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:02,000
as expressions of initiative

413
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
and we ought to praise them

414
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
and support them.

415
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
and we don't only do that with the scout,

416
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
but to our fellow adult volunteers

417
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,000
and the scouts, fellow

418
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
youth leaders,

419
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
even if they don't get everything out of it

420
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
that you think they ought to,

421
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:20,000
even if it kind of falls on its face a little bit,

422
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:22,000
even if they fail outright,

423
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:24,000
we ought to recognize initiative

424
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:26,000
every time and express

425
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:28,000
appreciation and our admiration

426
00:15:28,000 --> 00:15:30,000
for that.

427
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:32,000
that's a real big encouragement to scouts.

428
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
now. we also

429
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
have to understand that there will be,

430
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:38,000
at times, a decided

431
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:40,000
lack of initiative. it may

432
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:42,000
take some real digging to find

433
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
something, something,

434
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
the least little bit of anything.

435
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
in the absence of anything good to say,

436
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
take the bad

437
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
and contrast it with the worst.

438
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
you know you may have missed that,

439
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
but at least the worst didn't happen.

440
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:58,000
you know you didn't bring a

441
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,000
frying pan, but at least

442
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:02,000
you brought the eggs.

443
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
and if you can't even find

444
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
anything there, if all else

445
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
fails, remember that your

446
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
youth leaders are volunteering to be

447
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
scouts. they're showing up,

448
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
they're walking through the door,

449
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
even at the worst of times,

450
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
that's something to celebrate.

451
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
glad to see you, glad you're here.

452
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
let's get to work. once we've learned

453
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
to these little

454
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
signals of initiative, we need to

455
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
observe and reflect. it's important

456
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
to reiterate that scout masters

457
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
are not bosses or commanders.

458
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
we're supporting

459
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
facilitators. okay,

460
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
we recognize and respect the authority

461
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
and the responsibility of our youth leaders.

462
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
our chief activity is not actively

463
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
advising, directing and mentoring

464
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
every moment. our role is observing

465
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
and then reflecting.

466
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
observing and reflecting.

467
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
put yourself in the role of a coach.

468
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
stay on the sidelines and off the field,

469
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
let the scouts play the game

470
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
and then reflect on their performance.

471
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
to ask afterwards. and how do you do

472
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
that? well, you ask how things went,

473
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
what they think went well, what

474
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
they could improve in next time.

475
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
honest and useful reflection

476
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
with a group of scout age boys

477
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
can maybe kind of difficult,

478
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
but be patient,

479
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
keep it to a few minutes at the most.

480
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
boys are especially sensitive

481
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
to criticism. even that kind of

482
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
good natured ribbing that they do with each other

483
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
and we sometimes get into

484
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
it can be taken the wrong way.

485
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
so watch out. the point of

486
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
reflection is guiding youth

487
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
leaders to discover what's happening,

488
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
not to tell them what's

489
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
happening, and I think that's important.

490
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
let me repeat it: the point of

491
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
reflecting is guiding

492
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
youth leaders to discover what's happening,

493
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
not for us to tell them

494
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
what's happening. so it's not an after

495
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
action report that we read.

496
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
it's a discussion

497
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
in which they describe

498
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
and discover what's going on. and this is

499
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
going to work really well if you learn to

500
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
respect the discovery process.

501
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
trial and error

502
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
and reflection is going to show

503
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
youth leaders what they don't know or don't

504
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
understand. that kind

505
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
of experience. as a powerful teacher,

506
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
five minutes of trying things out

507
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
beats five hours

508
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
of us telling them how things work.

509
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
developing youth leaders

510
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
will understand, accept

511
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
and apply training much more

512
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
readily when they see a need

513
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
for it, rather than being

514
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
told that they need it.

515
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
give them the environment to try,

516
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
observe the action,

517
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
guide a reflection

518
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
and help them discover

519
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
what they did well

520
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
and what needs a little bit of work.

521
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
if we understand how youth leaders

522
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
develop, create and maintain

523
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
the conditions that foster that

524
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
development, understand

525
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
and maintain a useful relationship

526
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
between adult volunteers and youth leaders,

527
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
and develop the skills of observation

528
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
and reflection, we'll see

529
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
our youth leaders grow in competence

530
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
and effectiveness.

